In this lesson we'll look at how to plan a statistical investigation so your data actually answers the question you care about. A good investigation has five stages: pose, collect, organise, analyse, and report, and it all starts with the question. Make it specific and answerable with numbers. "How many hours do Year 7 students sleep on a school night?" works. "Is sleep good for you?" is too vague. Next, plan your data. Identify the population, the whole group you care about, and the sample, the smaller group you'll actually measure. Also decide on your variable, and whether it's discrete, like a count of siblings, or continuous, like height in centimetres. Then choose primary or secondary data. Primary is data you collect yourself, through a survey or experiment. Secondary comes from a source like the ABS or a published study, but remember it was collected for someone else's purpose, so double-check it really fits your question. And watch out for sample bias. Asking only your football team whether they like sport won't give you a representative answer. Now let's see it in action.